---
description: NestJS conventions for the Pharmacy ERP backend
globs: backend/src/**/*.ts
alwaysApply: false
---

## Module structure

- One feature per module: `feature.module.ts`, `feature.controller.ts`, `feature.service.ts`
- Register providers in `@Module({ providers, exports, imports })`; export services other modules need
- Import `PersistenceModule` / `PrismaModule` when accessing DB or persistence services
- Keep `AppModule` as wiring only — feature logic belongs in feature modules

```
src/
  feature/
    feature.module.ts
    feature.controller.ts
    feature.service.ts
    dto/
      create-feature.dto.ts
```

---

## Dependency injection

- Constructor injection only; use `private readonly` for injected dependencies
- Do not instantiate services manually (`new FeatureService()`)
- Inject interfaces/tokens only when the project already uses them; default to concrete `@Injectable()` classes

```typescript
@Injectable()
export class FeatureService {
  constructor(
    private readonly unitOfWork: UnitOfWorkService,
    private readonly requestContext: RequestContextService,
  ) {}
}
```

---

## Controllers

- Controllers are thin: validate input (DTO), delegate to service, return result
- Use standard HTTP decorators (`@Get`, `@Post`, `@Param`, `@Body`, `@Query`)
- Do **not** hand-build `{ success: true, data: … }` — the global `ResponseInterceptor` wraps responses
- Return `PaginatedResult` from list endpoints so the interceptor adds pagination metadata

---

## DTOs and validation

Global `ValidationPipe` is configured in [main.ts](backend/src/main.ts):

```typescript
new ValidationPipe({
  whitelist: true,
  transform: true,
  forbidNonWhitelisted: true,
})
```

- Every request body/query DTO uses class-validator decorators (`@IsString`, `@IsNotEmpty`, `@IsOptional`, `@Type`, etc.)
- Use `@Transform` for type coercion (e.g. string → `bigint`) when needed
- DTOs live in a `dto/` subfolder per feature
- Validation messages should be clear and user-facing

---

## Error handling

### ApplicationException

Business and domain errors use `ApplicationException`:

```typescript
throw new ApplicationException(
  ErrorCode.STOCK_INSUFFICIENT,
  'Insufficient stock for batch',
  HttpStatus.CONFLICT,
  { batchId, requestedQty },
);
```

- First arg: code from `ErrorCode` — add new codes to [error-code.ts](backend/src/common/exceptions/error-code.ts)
- Second arg: human-readable message
- Third arg: HTTP status (default `400`)
- Fourth arg: optional `details` payload for clients

### GlobalExceptionFilter

[GlobalExceptionFilter](backend/src/common/exceptions/global-exception.filter.ts) maps exceptions to:

```json
{ "success": false, "error": { "code": "…", "message": "…", "details": … } }
```

- Do not catch-and-swallow in services; let exceptions propagate to the filter
- Use Nest `HttpException` only for framework-level concerns; prefer `ApplicationException` for domain errors

---

## Response envelope

[ResponseInterceptor](backend/src/common/interceptors/response.interceptor.ts) wraps successful responses:

```json
{ "success": true, "data": … }
```

- Return raw data from controllers/services — the interceptor adds the envelope
- For paginated lists, return a `PaginatedResult` (detected by `isPaginatedResult()`)

---

## Logging

- Use Nest `Logger` with the class name: `private readonly logger = new Logger(MyService.name)`
- Log unexpected failures at `error` level with stack traces
- Do not log sensitive data (passwords, tokens)

---

## Lifecycle hooks

- `OnModuleInit` / `OnModuleDestroy` for connection setup/teardown (see [PrismaService](backend/src/prisma.service.ts))
- Keep lifecycle logic in the service that owns the resource

---

## Guards, interceptors, filters

- Register global filters/interceptors in `main.ts` or `AppModule` providers with `APP_FILTER` / `APP_INTERCEPTOR`
- Feature-specific guards/interceptors stay in the feature module

---

## Environment and config

- Use `@nestjs/config` for environment variables
- Access via `ConfigService`; do not read `process.env` directly in services (except bootstrap in `main.ts`)
